<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meiji era</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Meiji period, Meiji jidai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meiji era was the period of Japanese history from 23 October 1868 to 30 July 1912, the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a>. In it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> moved from an isolated feudal society at risk of colonisation by Western powers to an industrialised nation-state and emerging great power, adopting Western science, technology, law and political ideas; it opened with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a> of 1868 and gave Japan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889. Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> rebelled against the changes in the 1870s, most notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a>'s Satsuma Rebellion. Victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> in 1894–1895 and over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1904–1905 made Japan an international power. It was followed by the Taishō era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meiji-era.jpg" alt="Meiji era" width="330" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uchida Kuichi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meiji_tenno3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Prince Mutsuhito succeeded his father, Emperor Kōmei, on 3 February 1867 at the age of fourteen, as the 122nd emperor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> was under pressure to modernise, and a call arose for the restoration of governing power to the emperor. On 9 November 1867 the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> offered his resignation, and the new government was formed on 3 January 1868. The fall of Edo in the summer of 1868 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and brought the Meiji era. Its first reform was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a> of 1868, which called among other things for a deliberative assembly and for all classes to unite in carrying out the affairs of state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The leaders set out to catch up with the West through state-led industrialisation. The government built railways, began land reform and set up a Western-based education system, sending thousands of students abroad and hiring more than 3,000 foreign experts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission">Iwakura Mission</a> toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1871. Land and tax laws of 1871 to 1873 legalised private ownership and replaced taxes in kind with cash. Military reform brought a small standing army, a large reserve and compulsory militia service for all men.</p><p>A new peerage, the Kazoku, with five ranks from prince to baron, was created for about five hundred court nobles, former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itagaki-taisuke">Itagaki Taisuke</a> led the Freedom and People's Rights Movement for representative government, and helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> in 1881. In 1885 the essayist Fukuzawa Yukichi argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> should orient itself on the civilised countries of the West.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Rapid change had opponents. Disaffected former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> rebelled in the 1870s, the best known being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> in the Satsuma Rebellion, although other former samurai, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itagaki-taisuke">Itagaki Taisuke</a>, served the government. Fukuzawa's essay may also have laid intellectual foundations for later Japanese colonialism in the region.</p><p>The government promoted Japanese art abroad at world's fairs, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1873. The years 1890 to 1910 are called the golden age of Japanese cloisonné enamel, and Shibata Zeshin renewed lacquerwork. The emperor promoted Western dress, which became common in public, while the kimono returned in bolder styles in the 1890s. The era ended on 30 July 1912, and the Taishō era followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meiji era” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1774 Sugita Genpaku and his colleagues published the Kaitai Shinsho, a translation of a Dutch anatomical text, and by the early nineteenth century rangaku scholars had prepared intellectual foundations for the rapid modernisation of the <b>Meiji period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The word first meant a lord or a dear one; from the <b>Meiji era</b> to 1945 the song was understood as wishing the emperor a long reign, and since 1999 the government has read it as a wish for the lasting peace and prosperity of Japan under the…</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Kōjō no Tsuki&quot; (&quot;The Moon over the Ruined Castle&quot;) is a Japanese song of the <b>Meiji period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kojo-no-tsuki">Kōjō no Tsuki</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody has been popular since the <b>Meiji period</b>, and its words took their present form then.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sakura-sakura">Sakura Sakura</a></li></ul></section>
